1
00:00:06,280 --> 00:00:08,480
Row 1 of a cinema has 12 seats.

2
00:00:08,530 --> 00:00:12,500
Each row has 3 more seats than the row in front.

3
00:00:12,550 --> 00:00:15,050
How many seats are there in row 25?

4
00:00:15,100 --> 00:00:18,790
Pause the video and work it out.

5
00:00:18,840 --> 00:00:21,990
The difference is 3, so start with 3 n.

6
00:00:22,040 --> 00:00:25,530
3 times 1 is 3, but row 1 has 12, so add 9.

7
00:00:25,580 --> 00:00:27,970
The rule is 3 n plus 9.

8
00:00:28,020 --> 00:00:33,640
For row 25: 3 times 25 is 75, plus 9 is 84 seats.

9
00:00:33,690 --> 00:00:38,740
Remember: a position-to-term rule gives a term from its position.

10
00:00:38,790 --> 00:00:44,620
Term equals 3 times the position plus 9 gives 12, 15, 18, and so on.

11
00:00:44,670 --> 00:00:49,730
The n-th term is the position-to-term rule written in algebra.

12
00:00:49,780 --> 00:00:54,250
For 12, 15, 18, 21, the n-th term is 3 n plus 9.

13
00:00:54,300 --> 00:00:56,450
It lets you find any term.

14
00:00:56,500 --> 00:01:00,190
And it lets you find the position of a term.

15
00:01:00,240 --> 00:01:02,230
Which row has 105 seats?

16
00:01:02,280 --> 00:01:04,100
3 n plus 9 equals 105.

17
00:01:04,150 --> 00:01:06,650
3 n equals 96, so n equals 32.

18
00:01:06,700 --> 00:01:07,800
Row 32.

19
00:01:07,850 --> 00:01:08,600
Your turn.

20
00:01:08,650 --> 00:01:11,430
Find the n-th term of 2, 9, 16, 23.

21
00:01:11,480 --> 00:01:15,010
Pause the video and try it.

22
00:01:15,060 --> 00:01:17,210
The difference is 7, so 7 n.

23
00:01:17,260 --> 00:01:21,540
7 times 1 is 7, and the first term is 2, so subtract 5.

24
00:01:21,590 --> 00:01:24,370
The n-th term is 7 n minus 5.

25
00:01:24,420 --> 00:01:29,130
To find the first term above a value, solve an inequality.

26
00:01:29,180 --> 00:01:33,070
Which is the first row with more than 100 seats?

27
00:01:33,120 --> 00:01:37,910
3 n plus 9 is greater than 100, so n is greater than 30.33.

28
00:01:37,960 --> 00:01:41,000
That is row 31, with 102 seats.

29
00:01:41,050 --> 00:01:42,350
Here is the method.

30
00:01:42,400 --> 00:01:45,600
Step 1: find the common difference, d.

31
00:01:45,650 --> 00:01:50,530
Step 2: write d n, then add or subtract to match the first term.

32
00:01:50,580 --> 00:01:53,050
Step 3: substitute n to find terms.

33
00:01:53,100 --> 00:01:56,260
Solve an equation to find a position.

34
00:01:56,310 --> 00:01:59,330
Step 4: check with a term you know.

35
00:01:59,380 --> 00:02:03,590
Find the n-th term of negative 8, negative 3, 2, 7.

36
00:02:03,640 --> 00:02:05,940
The difference is 5, so 5 n.

37
00:02:05,990 --> 00:02:07,190
5 times 1 is 5.

38
00:02:07,240 --> 00:02:09,780
To get negative 8, subtract 13.

39
00:02:09,830 --> 00:02:12,840
The n-th term is 5 n minus 13.

40
00:02:12,890 --> 00:02:14,670
Which term is 132?

41
00:02:14,720 --> 00:02:17,110
5 n minus 13 equals 132.

42
00:02:17,160 --> 00:02:20,270
5 n equals 145, so n equals 29.

43
00:02:20,320 --> 00:02:22,850
It is the 29th term.

44
00:02:22,900 --> 00:02:23,640
Your turn.

45
00:02:23,690 --> 00:02:26,080
Which term of 3 n plus 9 is 90?

46
00:02:26,130 --> 00:02:29,210
Pause and work it out.

47
00:02:29,260 --> 00:02:31,030
3 n plus 9 equals 90.

48
00:02:31,080 --> 00:02:33,290
Subtract 9: 3 n equals 81.

49
00:02:33,340 --> 00:02:35,460
Divide by 3: n equals 27.

50
00:02:35,510 --> 00:02:40,010
It is the 27th term, just like the cinema row.

51
00:02:40,060 --> 00:02:44,950
Find the n-th term of 50, 46, 42, 38, and its 15th term.

52
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:47,500
The difference is negative 4.

53
00:02:47,550 --> 00:02:53,340
50 minus negative 4 is 54, so the n-th term is 54 minus 4 n.

54
00:02:53,390 --> 00:02:58,530
The 15th term is 54 minus 60, which is negative 6.

55
00:02:58,580 --> 00:03:02,020
Eva finds the n-th term of 9, 14, 19, 24.

56
00:03:02,070 --> 00:03:04,960
Which line goes wrong first?

57
00:03:05,010 --> 00:03:06,220
Line 2 is wrong.

58
00:03:06,270 --> 00:03:09,300
5 n plus 9 gives 14 when n is 1, not 9.

59
00:03:09,350 --> 00:03:13,470
She added the first term instead of comparing.

60
00:03:13,520 --> 00:03:14,760
5 times 1 is 5.

61
00:03:14,810 --> 00:03:16,130
To get 9, add 4.

62
00:03:16,180 --> 00:03:19,090
The n-th term is 5 n plus 4.

63
00:03:19,140 --> 00:03:21,350
Some taxi fares are a sequence.

64
00:03:21,400 --> 00:03:25,880
A ride starts at 4 dollars, and each kilometre adds 2 dollars.

65
00:03:25,930 --> 00:03:30,150
After n kilometres, the fare is 2 n plus 4 dollars.

66
00:03:30,200 --> 00:03:33,030
How far can you go for 30 dollars?

67
00:03:33,080 --> 00:03:37,800
2 n plus 4 equals 30 gives n equals 13 kilometres.

68
00:03:37,850 --> 00:03:39,270
One last question.

69
00:03:39,320 --> 00:03:42,460
What is the 20th term of 100 minus 3 n?

70
00:03:42,510 --> 00:03:46,060
Pause the video and answer.

71
00:03:46,110 --> 00:03:47,880
Put n equals 20.

72
00:03:47,930 --> 00:03:53,100
3 times 20 is 60, and 100 minus 60 is 40.

73
00:03:53,150 --> 00:03:59,960
To remember: the n-th term of a linear sequence is d n plus c, where c is the first term minus d.

74
00:04:00,010 --> 00:04:02,530
Substitute to find terms.

75
00:04:02,580 --> 00:04:06,420
Solve the n-th term equal to a value to find its position.

76
00:04:06,470 --> 00:04:09,980
A whole number n means it is in the sequence.

77
00:04:10,030 --> 00:04:14,060
Use inequalities to find the first term over a value.

78
00:04:14,110 --> 00:04:19,150
Great work! Now practise lesson 9.3 on Camday.
